  "body": "This supplies an explicit finite-width modular backend for PR1057's borrowed-port echo. Integer matrix factors execute modulo `2^w`, restore every dirty bit pattern, and recover the exact signed output from input/output bounds. Large intermediate integer lifts need not fit the data registers. **Recursive-interface construction; no new kappa.**\n\nFor the 52-factor fixture, the existing plan still takes **1,376 complete leaf calls and three dirty length-two arrays**, using **56-bit data**. The inherited integer-prefix majorant gives 114 sufficient bits for entry values bounded by seven, or 167 for arbitrary signed 56-bit dirt. These are sufficient-bound comparisons, not integer-width minima. The retained arbitrary-dirt integer lift actually reaches **90 signed bits**.\n\nThe cost is explicit: **38 scalar/access commands per two-dimensional leaf instead of 28**, including ten reductions. The full kernel executes **52,292 commands**, of which **13,760 are reductions**. All five arrays occupy 560 model bits; the four private work registers occupy **283 bits**, including a 112-bit product register. Coefficient/input conversion, final decoding, original input storage, planning, metadata, routing and physical adapters have separate paid terms. No measured speedup follows from this invoice.\n\n## Reproduce and evidence\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/modular-port-borrowing/verify.py\npython3 -I -B research/modular-port-borrowing/test_package.py -v\n```\n\nPython 3.10+ and standard library only. Expected marker: `PASS_MODULAR_PORT_BORROWING`.\n\n- **5,280 exact matrix cases** covering all ten input columns: all 861 ordered binary/ternary trees through seven factors at three widths and both signs, plus each parent plan from eight through 64 in both directions.\n- A separate control-stack and matrix implementation checks **528,302 leaf prefixes and 667,416 recursive returns**, including borrowed ancestor ports.\n- **33,280 exhaustive scalar cases** over the declared small quotient rings execute **2,160,640 actual scalar/access commands**; another **144 dense cases** vary dimension, signs, zero factors and width.\n- Four primary executions compare every leaf with the integer lift, restore arbitrary dirty bits and replay the literal inverse. Closed operation and address invoices agree with literal execution.\n- **19 mathematical/admission controls, 12 package tests and 25 existing selected-record tests** pass. Final normal CPython 3.14.4 and read-only, relocated, isolated optimized 3.12.3 receipts are byte-identical.\n- Recovery failure is explicit: residue 12 modulo 16 decodes as \u22124 when the true result is 12 and the bound is omitted. Saturation corrupts a helper; rounded division fails the quotient contract. The guard separately preserves input representability when a zero product makes the output small.\n\n## Scope and credit\n\nOnly `research/modular-port-borrowing/` is added. Base **ba82555c64e1879e4b7decce28186f208fcb8262**, OS seed **52**. Six exact Git-object/SHA-256 source/context pins include five unchanged PR1057 files at **78a062df1dcab2bffea5ff4d8243f3da6e465414**. The generator/planner and additive-group identity remain credited predecessor work; the contribution is their concrete quotient-register backend, recovery guard and full cost/validation evidence.\n\nThe all-tree argument applies to **integer linear factors** and residue-valued recursive boundaries. Rounded, irrational or general dyadic leaves need another interface. The Python interpreter models bounded registers; its host allocation is not the stated data-space bound. A physical fixed-tape compiler, supplier/frame adapters, hosting and outer analytic transfer remain open. Leaf multiplicities and the direct all-role obstruction are unchanged. No supplier admission, full repository suite, selected cold replay, CI success or formal multiplication theorem is claimed.\n\nThe final queue was checked through **#1157**. **Ali Romman's #1135** was read through its exact-lift proof: it studies rounded restoration and a common-denominator rational embedding with `q^D` initialization and numerator headroom. This integer modular backend does not remove its denominator costs or solve its rounded-leaf obstruction. #1103's rectangular batching, #1155's deferred batch restoration and #835/#1144's modular verification have different objects and are acknowledged. This backend restores every completed child boundary and does not defer those restorations. Upstream advanced to **ce7ad1b6ab09452c3478723a2e96ed5ff212dc08**, selecting conditional PR1094; the experimental context remains frozen.\n\nCredit Gareth Davidson PR1057, shhxera PR822, androolloyd PR673, Sagar Thakrar PR730, Chafik Boukhalfa PR628 and the retained lineage. The Ben-Or\u2013Cleve register principle and modular arithmetic are classical; no priority claim is made for them. Prepared by **Gareth Davidson (@bitplane)** with substantial **OpenAI Codex assistance**; new material Apache-2.0.\n",
